## Diagnosis
The current bug report describes that calling the `iterator` method of `ListPopulation` returns an iterator of the modifiable list without wrapping it in an unmodifiable list. This allows users to remove chromosomes from the population, which is unintended behavior.

Looking at the failing test `testIterator` in `ListPopulationTest`, the test creates a `ListPopulation` instance and adds some chromosomes to it. It then retrieves an iterator from the population and tries to remove elements using `iter.remove()`. This removal behavior is the issue reported in the bug.

Inspecting the code snippet of the `ListPopulation` class would provide more insights into how the iterator is constructed and returned without being wrapped in an unmodifiable list.

## Code Inspection
Snippet of `ListPopulation` class:
```java
public class ListPopulation implements Population {

    protected List<Chromosome> chromosomes;

    public ListPopulation(int populationLimit) {
        chromosomes = new ArrayList<>(populationLimit);
    }

    public void addChromosomes(List<Chromosome> chromosomeList) {
        this.chromosomes.addAll(chromosomeList);
    }

    public Iterator<Chromosome> iterator() {
        return chromosomes.iterator();
    }

    // Other methods...
}
```

## Conclusion
The issue occurs because the `iterator` method of `ListPopulation` directly returns the iterator of the modifiable list `chromosomes`, allowing users to modify the population by removing elements. To fix this issue, the `iterator` method should return an iterator of an unmodifiable list to prevent modification of the population through the iterator.

## Suggested Fix
`ListPopulation.iterator()` should be modified to return an iterator of an unmodifiable list to prevent removal of chromosomes from the population inadvertently.
```java
ListPopulation.iterator() {
    return Collections.unmodifiableList(chromosomes).iterator();
}
```

